<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!--
    The state, action and chrome marks of the icon system (docs/design/handoff.md §"Shared
    components", docs/design/mockups.md mockup 2b).

    The law, verbatim: "16px grid, 1.5px stroke, square caps, geometry only."

    Four rules follow from it, and every key below obeys them:

    1. Every mark is authored inside a 16x16 coordinate box, origin top-left, y down. The one
       exception is `IconScanning`, which is authored in its own 14x14 box - see its comment.
       The painted result, stroke included, never leaves the box: a ring of radius 7 centred on
       (8,8) is drawn with a 1.5px pen, so its outer edge lands at 0.25 and 15.75.
    2. There is no colour here, and there must never be one. A geometry is a shape; the call
       site names the token it is painted with (StatusOkBrush for connected, StatusErrorBrush for
       cannot-access, StatusAdvisoryBrush for the advisory, AccentBrush for scanning, and so on).
       A hex literal in this file would be a bug even though the file lives under Themes/.
    3. Stroke thickness and caps are the consuming control's job, not the geometry's:
       `StrokeThickness="{StaticResource IconStrokeThickness}"` (1.5) and
       `StrokeLineCap="Square"`. Nothing is baked in, so one pen change restyles every mark.
       The tiny dots of `!`, `i` and `?` are short stroke segments that rely on that square cap
       to read as dots - with a flat cap they degrade to small dashes rather than disappearing.
    4. Placement comes from the authored box, never from a mark's own extents. A control draws
       these at `Stretch="None"` with Width/Height set to `IconSize` (16) - or, if it scales them
       to some larger size, by mapping the *box* (16, or 14 for the spinner) onto that size.
       Fitting a mark's own `Geometry.Bounds` into the box instead is the one thing that must not
       happen: it is what makes a grid worth having. Two marks here fail loudly under it -
       `IconConnectedCore` is a 5.6-wide dot that would be inflated to fill the whole box, and
       `IconScanning`'s bounds are deliberately lop-sided (its right quadrant is missing), so
       fitting them re-centres the arc and the spinner then wobbles as it turns. The rest fail
       quietly, by drifting a fraction off the centre the whole family shares.

    Fill vs stroke, per key (the whole contract in one table):

      IconConnected      stroke only  - filling it turns the ring into a disc
      IconConnectedCore  fill only    - the centre dot of the connected mark
      IconNotDetected    stroke only, and dashed by the pen
      IconCannotAccess   stroke only
      IconWarning        stroke only
      IconError          stroke only
      IconInformation    stroke only
      IconConfirmation   stroke only
      IconEject          fill only    - a solid triangle over a solid bar
      IconClose          stroke only
      IconRefresh        stroke only
      IconExternalLink   stroke only
      IconOption         stroke only
      IconScanning       stroke only
      IconDragHandle     fill only    - six dots; stroking them would draw six rings
      IconInsert         stroke only

    Sizing hints are not re-declared here: `IconSize`, `IconStrokeThickness`, `SpinnerSize` and
    `SpinnerStrokeThickness` already live in Themes/Geometry.axaml.

    Geometry is theme-independent - a shape does not change when the OS flips to light - so this
    is a plain ResourceDictionary with no ThemeDictionaries, exactly like Geometry.axaml.
  -->

  <!-- ===== The shared ring ==============================================================
       Every state mark is built on the same circle: centre (8,8), radius 7, which is the
       specimen's 14px ring (1.5px border, 5px core) scaled onto the 16px grid. It is written
       out per key rather than shared through an alias so that any one mark can diverge later
       without disturbing the others. -->

  <!-- Connected. The specimen is a stroked ring with a filled core dot, and one geometry cannot
       be both: filling this path would fill the ring solid. The mark is therefore split in two -
       `IconConnected` is the ring the control strokes, `IconConnectedCore` the dot it fills -
       and a connected mark is two Paths in one 16x16 cell, sharing the centre (8,8). -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconConnected">M 1,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 15,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 1,8 Z</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- The core dot: radius 2.8, the specimen's 5px dot on the 16px grid. Fill it; do not stroke
       it, or it grows by the pen width in every direction. It shares the ring's centre and must
       be drawn at the ring's scale - this is the mark that proves rule 4 above, because a control
       that fits a geometry to its own bounds would draw this 5.6-wide dot as a 16-wide disc. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconConnectedCore">M 5.2,8 A 2.8,2.8 0 1 1 10.8,8 A 2.8,2.8 0 1 1 5.2,8 Z</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Not detected: the ring alone, no core. The design draws it *dashed*, and a dash pattern is
       a property of the pen, not of the shape - so this is the plain circle and the call site
       adds `StrokeDashArray="3,2"` (dashes are measured in stroke widths: 4.5px on, 3px off,
       which is the ~3x rule a browser applies to the specimen's `1.5px dashed`). The
       circumference does not divide evenly by that period, so the pattern meets itself at the
       start point; at 16px the seam is invisible. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconNotDetected">M 1,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 15,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 1,8 Z</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Cannot access: the ring plus a centred exclamation mark - a stem from y 4.4 to 8.8 and a
       dot below it. The dot is a 0.6-long segment whose square caps square it off; painted, the
       whole mark spans 3.65 to 12.45, centred on the ring. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconCannotAccess">M 1,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 15,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 1,8 Z M 8,4.4 L 8,8.8 M 8,11.1 L 8,11.7</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- The advisory mark. Its geometry is today identical to `IconCannotAccess` - the design
       draws both as a `!` in a circle and separates them by colour, amber for the advisory and
       red for the failure, per the law that an advisory is never red. It is written out as its
       own key rather than aliased because the two say different things and are expected to
       diverge; a call site must name the one it means, so that a later change to the advisory
       shape cannot silently restyle every cannot-access mark in the app. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconWarning">M 1,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 15,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 1,8 Z M 8,4.4 L 8,8.8 M 8,11.1 L 8,11.7</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Error: the ring plus a centred cross, arms 2.6 either side of (8,8). -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconError">M 1,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 15,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 1,8 Z M 5.4,5.4 L 10.6,10.6 M 10.6,5.4 L 5.4,10.6</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Information: the ring plus a lowercase `i`, which is the exclamation mark inverted - the
       dot on top (a 0.6-long segment) and the stem below it. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconInformation">M 1,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 15,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 1,8 Z M 8,4.3 L 8,4.9 M 8,7.2 L 8,11.6</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Confirmation: the ring plus a question mark. The hook is a 270-degree arc around (8,6.2)
       at radius 2 - it starts at the bowl's left, runs clockwise over the top and down the right
       side to the bottom, then drops straight into the stem - and the dot below it is the same
       0.6-long segment the other marks use. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconConfirmation">M 1,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 15,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 1,8 Z M 6,6.2 A 2,2 0 1 1 8,8.2 L 8,8.9 M 8,11.2 L 8,11.8</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- ===== Action and chrome marks ====================================================== -->

  <!-- Eject: a solid triangle pointing up over a solid bar. The specimen is a 10x6 triangle, a
       2px gap and an 11x2 bar; scaled onto the 16px grid that is 11.4x6.8, a 2.3 gap and a
       12.6x2.3 bar, which the design bottom-aligns in its box and this centres in the icon box
       (the mark spans y 2.3 to 13.7, centred on (8,8) like every ring above, so an eject mark
       sits on the same baseline as the state marks beside it).

       This one is filled, not stroked: both subpaths are closed regions, and stroking them would
       fatten the triangle by a pen width and turn the bar into an outlined box. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconEject">M 8,2.3 L 13.7,9.1 L 2.3,9.1 Z M 1.7,11.4 L 14.3,11.4 L 14.3,13.7 L 1.7,13.7 Z</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Close: the bare cross a dismiss affordance is drawn with - a toast's "x", and nothing that
       announces a state. Two strokes crossing on (8,8), arms 4 either side, so the mark spans half
       its box the way the rest of the set's diagonals do.

       This is deliberately NOT `IconError`, which is the same cross *inside the shared ring*: the
       ring is what makes that mark a state, and a dismiss button wearing it would read as "this
       toast is an error" rather than "close this". The two are separate keys for the same reason
       `IconWarning` and `IconCannotAccess` are - a call site must name the one it means. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconClose">M 4,4 L 12,12 M 12,4 L 4,12</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Refresh: the static rescan mark - a circular arrow. Centre (8,8), radius 5; the arc runs
       clockwise 290 degrees (hence the large-arc flag) from the 25-degree position round to 315,
       leaving one gap on the right. At 315 the tangent is exactly 45 degrees, so the arc runs on
       into a straight 3.5-long stub in that direction and the head is the square corner at its end,
       one arm 3.3 to the left and one 3.3 up - the arrow's vertex pointing down and right, along
       the direction of travel and across the gap.

       Three numbers here are the pen's doing rather than the drawing's, and this mark was drawn
       twice before they were right:

       - The arms are 3.3 long, better than two pen widths. At 2.4 - the length that looks correct
         on paper - a 1.5 stroke paints them as one blob and the head reads as a wart on the ring.
       - The vertex is pushed *outside* the circle by the stub. A chevron hung straight off the
         arc's end has its inner arm running alongside the arc about a pen width away, and the two
         strokes merge into a solid wedge; 3.5 of tangent is what buys the arm its clearance.
       - The arc starts at 25 degrees rather than at 3 o'clock, which is what keeps its first point
         3.5 clear of the vertex. Closer, and the pen closes the gap and the mark reads as a ring
         with something stuck to it rather than as an arrow chasing its own tail.

       Not `IconScanning`, and it must not become it: that mark is the *busy* spinner, authored
       lop-sided in its own 14x14 box so it can rotate without wobbling, and spoken for by the
       `.spinner` style. This one never moves - it is the face of a button the user presses - so it
       is drawn balanced on the 16px grid, its ink centred within a fraction of (8,8). -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconRefresh">M 12.53,10.11 A 5,5 0 1 1 11.54,4.46 L 14.01,6.94 M 10.71,6.94 L 14.01,6.94 L 14.01,3.64</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- External link: the north-east arrow that ends a link leaving the app ("Troubleshooting tips
       ↗"). A diagonal shaft from (4,12) to (12,4) and an L-shaped head at the tip - along the top
       and down the right - which the mitre join keeps square. It spans the same 4..12 as
       `IconClose`, so the two read at one weight when they sit in the same row.

       It exists as geometry because the law leaves no alternative: no Latin-1 character may stand
       in for an icon, so the arrow a link ends with is a mark like any other. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconExternalLink">M 4,12 L 12,4 M 7,4 L 12,4 L 12,9</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Option: the `⌥` of the layer switcher's `⌥1`-`⌥5` legends. It exists for exactly the reason
       `IconExternalLink` does - U+2325 OPTION KEY is in none of the seven embedded IBM Plex faces,
       so typing it draws tofu - and it is a mark rather than a substitute word because the legend
       names a key the user physically presses.

       The shape is NOT invented. It is traced from the outlines macOS itself ships: the glyph was
       pulled out of `.SFNS-Regular` (the system UI face), `Menlo-Regular`, `Lucida Grande` and
       `Arial Unicode MS` with CoreText, and all four agree on one skeleton, so this follows it:

         - a horizontal stub at the TOP LEFT,
         - a diagonal falling from the end of that stub to the BOTTOM RIGHT,
         - a horizontal stub along the bottom, to the right edge,
         - and a DETACHED bar at the top right, sitting above the bottom stub and overhanging its
           left end slightly.

       It descends left-to-right; a version that rises would be a different (and wrong) picture.

       The proportions are the four faces' averages mapped onto the 16px grid: ink spans x 2..14 and
       y 4..12, centred on (8,8) like every other mark, so the option mark sits on the same baseline
       as the marks beside it. The diagonal is 61 degrees from the horizontal (the faces run 50-63).
       The three runs are 3.6 / 4.0 / 4.8 long against Menlo's 0.69 / 0.81 / 1.0 ratios. Stroked, at
       the shared 1.5 pen; the square caps are what square off the four open ends.

       Drawn at `Size` 12, not 16 - see `Icon.kbdMark` in Styles/Editor.axaml for why. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconOption">M 2,4 L 5.6,4 L 10,12 L 14,12 M 9.2,4 L 14,4</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Scanning: the busy spinner, three quarters of a ring with the right quadrant open (the
       specimen is a 14px circle with `border-right-color: transparent`). The arc runs from the
       1:30 position anti-clockwise over the top, down the left and along the bottom to 4:30.

       This is the one mark NOT on the 16px grid: it is authored in a 14x14 box, centred on
       (7,7) at radius 6.25, because `SpinnerSize` is 14 and the spinner control sizes itself to
       that token. Keeping the geometry's centre on the control's centre is what makes the
       rotation true - a 14px mark parked inside a 16px box would spin about a point 1px off its
       own centre unless the control compensated. Draw it at Width/Height `SpinnerSize` with
       `Stretch="None"`, stroke `SpinnerStrokeThickness`, and rotate about (0.5, 0.5) for
       `DurationSpinnerRotation` (1.1s, linear, infinite - see Themes/Motion.axaml). -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconScanning">M 11.42,2.58 A 6.25,6.25 0 1 0 11.42,11.42</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Drag handle: the grip on a reorderable row (mockup 2i draws it as U+283F, "drag ⠿ · ⌥↑↓").
       Six dots on two columns at x=6 and x=10 and three rows at y=4, 8 and 12, each a circle of
       radius 1 - so the whole grip spans 5..11 across and 3..13 down, comfortably inside the box
       and read at a glance as "grab me".

       IT IS GEOMETRY BECAUSE IT HAS TO BE. U+283F is in NEITHER embedded IBM Plex family, so the
       character would fall through to a platform substitute and tofu where there is none - the same
       reason the record dot is an Ellipse and mockup 1e's `⌕` became a Path.

       FILL ONLY. Stroking it would draw six thin rings instead of six dots. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconDragHandle">M 5,4 A 1,1 0 1 0 7,4 A 1,1 0 1 0 5,4 M 9,4 A 1,1 0 1 0 11,4 A 1,1 0 1 0 9,4 M 5,8 A 1,1 0 1 0 7,8 A 1,1 0 1 0 5,8 M 9,8 A 1,1 0 1 0 11,8 A 1,1 0 1 0 9,8 M 5,12 A 1,1 0 1 0 7,12 A 1,1 0 1 0 5,12 M 9,12 A 1,1 0 1 0 11,12 A 1,1 0 1 0 9,12</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Insert: the `＋` of the step editor's trailing "＋ insert step" row (mockup 2i). Two 8-unit
       strokes crossing at the centre, spanning the same 4..12 as `IconClose` and
       `IconExternalLink`, so the three read at one weight in a column of rows.

       It is deliberately NOT `IconClose` rotated: they are the same two segments at different
       angles, and a `+` that was authored as an `×` would be a dismiss mark wherever a style forgot
       to rotate it. U+FF0B, the mock's own character, is in neither embedded family. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconInsert">M 8,4 L 8,12 M 4,8 L 12,8</StreamGeometry>
</ResourceDictionary>
